PLACES=[
N('fort-worden-wa','Fort Worden','Port Townsend','wa','fort','standing',48.13248,-122.76365,'site','public','walk','forts','1898–1953',
  'A Coast Artillery post built between 1898 and 1920 on Point Wilson, one of three forts (with Fort Flagler and Fort Casey) whose guns made a "triangle of fire" across Admiralty Inlet, the sea entrance to Puget Sound. Concrete for the batteries was mixed with cement shipped from Belgium round Cape Horn; when finished the post had 41 guns and mortars in twelve batteries. The guns never fired at an enemy: many went to Europe in the First World War and the rest were scrapped in the Second. The Army left in 1953, the state used the post as a juvenile detention centre, and it opened as a state park in 1973. It is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'The empty concrete batteries along the bluff (Randol, Kinzie and the rest), open to explore until dusk; no guns or mounts are left. The parade ground with Officers\' Row, the 1920s balloon hangar, and Alexander\'s Castle, a brick house of 1883 older than the fort. The Commanding Officer\'s Quarters at 1 Pershing Avenue is a house museum set up as an officer\'s home of 1904 to 1953, with almost every room open; it is free, open 11 to 4, Friday to Tuesday from April to October and Saturdays from November to March (park page read November 2026). The Puget Sound Coast Artillery Museum is also in the park; its hours were not checked.',
  'Twelve concrete gun batteries you can walk through, built to stop a fleet that never came.',
  'Fort Worden Historical State Park, run by Washington State Parks. Open 6:30 a.m. to dusk in summer and 8 a.m. to dusk in winter (park page read November 2026). '+DP+' The park office is in building 202. Dogs on leash; the park warns that cougars are seen now and then. The boat launch is closed. Part of the beach is owned by the Port of Port Townsend.',
  [W('Fort Worden'),L(WSP+': Fort Worden','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-worden-historical-state-park'),L(WSP+': Commanding Officer\'s Quarters Museum','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-worden-historical-state-park/commanding-officers-quarters-museum')],
  th=[48.13493,-122.76596],thn='Battery Way lot',brick='some'),
N('point-wilson-light','Point Wilson Light','Port Townsend','wa','light','standing',48.14417,-122.75528,'exact','view','walk','','1879; tower 1914',
  'The first light, of 1879, was a wooden tower on the roof of a Cape Cod keeper\'s house, partner to the Admiralty Head light across the inlet. The sea kept eating the point, and in 1914 a new 46-foot octagonal tower of reinforced concrete was finished; it took over the station\'s original fourth-order Fresnel lens, and at 51 feet above the water it was the tallest light on Puget Sound. The old house lost its tower and stayed on as keepers\' quarters. The light was automated in 1976 and is still in use.',
  'The concrete tower and the 1879 keeper\'s house at the tip of the point, reached along the beach from Fort Worden. The Peninsula Daily News reported a celebration of the restored 1879 Fresnel lens being put back in service. The keeper\'s house is let as a holiday rental by the U.S. Lighthouse Society. Its own page said in November 2026 that weekend tours are suspended for the winter; summer tour days and times not checked.',
  'The tallest light on Puget Sound, still turning its 1879 lens.',
  'The Coast Guard owns the station and keeps the light; the U.S. Lighthouse Society leases the rest for $1 a year (Peninsula Daily News). State Parks says the light is reached only through Fort Worden but is not on park land; park in the Fort Worden beach lots (Discover Pass) and walk out. Outside tour days, look from the beach.',
  [W('Point Wilson Light'),L('Point Wilson Lighthouse (U.S. Lighthouse Society)','https://www.pointwilsonlighthouse.org/'),L('Peninsula Daily News: lighthouse leased to nonprofit','https://www.peninsuladailynews.com/news/point-wilson-lighthouse-leased-to-nonprofit'),L('Peninsula Daily News: restored lens','https://www.peninsuladailynews.com/news/restored-lens-to-be-celebrated-at-point-wilson/')],
  brick='no'),
N('fort-flagler','Fort Flagler','Nordland','wa','fort','standing',48.09505,-122.69478,'site','public','walk','forts','1897–1953',
  'The first of the three Admiralty Inlet forts to be built, on a 590-acre reservation set aside in 1866 at the north tip of Marrowstone Island. The Pacific Bridge Company of Portland began clearing old-growth timber in 1897, losing labourers to the Klondike gold rush, and the post was activated on July 27, 1899 with 26 guns and mortars. It trained soldiers for both world wars and for Korea, and closed on June 30, 1953. The state bought it between 1957 and 1962; a youth symphony held its summer music camp here from 1958 to 1989.',
  'The main gun line of concrete batteries on the bluff (a self-guided tour starts at the panels by the museum), with two 3-inch guns brought from Fort Wint in the Philippines in 1963 and mounted in Battery Wansboro. The hospital, the non-commissioned officers\' quarters and the barracks. The museum is in the old quartermaster storehouse, free, open 11 to 4: Wednesday to Sunday in April, May, September and October, every day June to August, and Saturdays November to March. Gun emplacement tours and a hospital open house are run in season.',
  'The fort that lost its builders to the Klondike gold rush.',
  'Fort Flagler Historical State Park, at the end of Flagler Road (SR 116), run by Washington State Parks. Open 6:30 a.m. to dusk in summer, 8 a.m. to dusk in winter. '+DP+' The park said in November 2026 that several historical buildings are closed for repairs through April 2027. Parking in front of the museum; phone (360) 385-1259.',
  [W('Fort Flagler State Park'),L(WSP+': Fort Flagler','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-flagler-historical-state-park'),L(WSP+': Fort Flagler history','https://parks.wa.gov/about/news-center/field-guide-blog/fort-flagler-historical-state-park-history'),L(WSP+': Fort Flagler Museum','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-flagler-historical-state-park/fort-flagler-museum')],
  brick='?'),
N('fort-townsend-wa','Fort Townsend','Port Townsend','wa','fort','traces',48.07494,-122.78898,'area','public','walk','forts','1856–1895',
  'One of the first US Army posts on the Salish Sea, set up on October 26, 1856 on Lt. Col. Silas Casey\'s orders, on land the S\'Klallam had not yet ceded (the Point No Point treaty was ratified only in 1859). Its troops went to San Juan Island in the "Pig War" of 1859 and after the Nez Perce in 1877, and inspectors said again and again that it was badly placed. A kerosene lamp set fire to the barracks, and the troops left on March 6, 1895. The state park opened in 1953; most of it is now protected old forest.',
  'Almost nothing: the park says the fort has all but returned to nature. A 0.3-mile interpretive trail loops round the old parade ground from the day-use lot, with signs on the post\'s history, and the tall flagpole, which was taken to a CCC camp in 1935 and returned for the fort\'s centenary in 1956. The Navy\'s brick "Torpedo X-Ray Tower" of the Second World War stands on former fort land; where it is and whether it can be seen was not checked.',
  'An 1850s frontier fort the forest has taken back.',
  'Fort Townsend Historical State Park, run by Washington State Parks, south of Port Townsend off State Route 20. Open 8 a.m. to dusk all year. '+DP+' Dogs on leash. The pin is the day-use lot where the history trail begins.',
  [L(WSP+': Fort Townsend','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-townsend-historical-state-park'),L(WSP+': Fort Townsend history','https://parks.wa.gov/about/news-center/field-guide-blog/fort-townsend-historical-state-park-history')],
  brick='?'),
N('jefferson-county-courthouse-wa','Jefferson County Courthouse','Port Townsend','wa','civic','standing',48.11217,-122.76780,'exact','view','drive','','1892',
  'A Romanesque Revival courthouse of 1892 by W. A. Ritchie, who also designed Spokane County\'s. The Register form says eleven stonecutters and twelve brick masons carved the stone and laid about four million bricks. Its 143-foot clock tower is seen from the water and has served ships as a landmark; the clock came round Cape Horn.',
  'Red brick and grey stone, carved column capitals, and the tall clock and bell tower. The form (1973) describes cedar wainscoting in the corridors. It is a working county courthouse; public hours and what can be seen inside were not checked.',
  'A clock tower built as much for ships as for the town.',
  'Owned and used by Jefferson County. Seen from the street; street parking round the building. Public hours not checked.',
  [W('Jefferson County Courthouse (Washington)'),NR('73001871')],
  brick='yes'),
N('port-townsend-city-hall','Port Townsend City Hall (Museum of Art and History)','Port Townsend','wa','civic','standing',48.11595,-122.75337,'exact','public','walk','','1891',
  'Port Townsend voted an $85,000 bond for a city hall on June 2, 1891, and the building went up that year to designs by the firm of Batwell and Patrick, of native fir, stone and bricks from a local yard. The town\'s boom collapsed almost at once, and the building was not paid off until January 1, 1947. The third floor and tower were later taken down and replaced by a flat roof. The Register form gives 1891; the historical society\'s museum page calls it the 1892 City Hall.',
  'Brick and stone front on Water Street. The old city jail in the basement was kept in its original state as a museum exhibit (form of 1971; whether it is shown now not checked). The building holds the Jefferson County Historical Society\'s Museum of Art and History, open Thursday to Sunday, 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. (page read November 2026); the first Saturday of the month is free, open until 7. Admission fees not checked; in season a ticket also covers the Rothschild House.',
  'A boom-town city hall that took 56 years to pay for.',
  'Museum of Art and History, 540 Water Street, run by the Jefferson County Historical Society; phone 360-385-1003 during open hours. Free two-hour parking on Water, Madison and Washington streets.',
  [NR('71000868'),L('Jefferson County Historical Society: Museum of Art and History','https://www.jchsmuseum.org/museum')],
  brick='?'),
N('rothschild-house-wa','Rothschild House','Port Townsend','wa','landmark','standing',48.11617,-122.75761,'exact','public','walk','','1868',
  'D.C.H. Rothschild, a Bavarian merchant who kept "The Kentucky Store" on a Port Townsend pier, built this plain Greek Revival house in 1868; the historical society names the builder as Horace Tucker. His widow Dorette, and then their daughter Emilie until 1954, kept it as it had been, heated by fireplaces and lit by gas. Their son Eugene gave it with its contents to the state in 1959, and it opened to visitors in 1962. Sources differ on when Rothschild arrived: 1858 (State Parks) or 1859 (the historical society).',
  'A house with its own furniture and belongings: the 1891 scroll wallpaper in the hall, Douglas fir hand-grained to look like oak, a curved Honduran mahogany banister, the back-to-back fireplace, and a stone-walled cellar. Open Thursday to Saturday, 11 to 4, April to September; adults $12, seniors and military $9, under-18s free (pages read November 2026). The interior is up steps and stairs, not wheelchair accessible.',
  'A merchant\'s house left almost untouched since the 1800s, down to the wallpaper.',
  'A Washington State Parks heritage site at 418 Taylor Street, opened by the Jefferson County Historical Society. The grounds are open all year until dusk. Limited free parking in the small lot on site; no public restroom.',
  [W('Rothschild House (Port Townsend, Washington)'),L(WSP+': The Rothschild House','https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-worden-historical-state-park/rothschild-house'),L(WSP+': Rothschild House history','https://parks.wa.gov/about/news-center/field-guide-blog/rothschild-house-state-park-heritage-site-history'),L('Jefferson County Historical Society: Rothschild House','https://jchsmuseum.org/visit/historic-houses/')],
  brick='?'),
N('leader-building-port-townsend','Leader Building (Fowler Building)','Port Townsend','wa','civic','standing',48.1153,-122.7542,'site','view','walk','','1874',
  'Built in 1874 for Captain Enoch S. Fowler, a Maine seaman who made Port Townsend his home port in 1852 and built its first safe landing wharf in 1859. The Register form calls it the oldest two-storey all-stone building in Washington. It is of sandstone quarried at Scow Bay on Kilisut Harbor; the newspaper the Leader later gave it its name.',
  'The sandstone front with its original 1874 name and date plate high up. The other two walls were later coated with cement stucco against weathering (form, 1970). Interior not checked.',
  'The oldest all-stone two-storey building in the state, says its Register form.',
  'A private building on Adams Street downtown; seen from the street. Street parking downtown.',
  [NR('70000637')],
  brick='no'),
N('port-townsend-customs-house','Customs House and Post Office','Port Townsend','wa','civic','standing',48.11344,-122.76199,'exact','public','walk','','1889–1893',
  'Congress bought the block on the bluff in 1885, and work on the basement began in 1889, when Port Townsend expected a railroad and a great future. The bank financing the railroad failed in November 1890, the boom collapsed, and the plans were cut back (the towers were built lower than drawn). The Leader called it "the handsomest and most costly post office on the Pacific Coast" when it opened on March 1, 1893, by which time the town had shrunk to under 2,000 people.',
  'A Richardson Romanesque front of quarry-faced sandstone over brick, with a round tower at one corner and a square one at the other and a tall arched entry. Still the main post office (it was in 1991); lobby hours not checked.',
  'The grandest post office on the coast, finished after the town that wanted it had emptied.',
  'United States Postal Service. Open to the public in post-office hours (not checked). Street parking.',
  [NR('91000652')],
  brick='no'),
N('port-townsend-carnegie-library','Port Townsend Carnegie Library','Port Townsend','wa','civic','standing',48.11658,-122.76348,'exact','public','walk','','1913',
  'The town library began in 1889 as the Young Ladies\' Reading Club. With a grant from Andrew Carnegie, a stucco library designed by Harold R. Ginnold was built on the bluff and opened on October 14, 1913. After a two-year closure the city library moved back into it in July 2014, its upstairs restored as one open public reading room.',
  'A stucco building reached by three long flights of concrete steps; inside, the upstairs reading room with a brick fireplace at each end (Port Townsend Leader). It is the working public library; hours not checked.',
  'A Carnegie reading room still in use, up three flights of steps.',
  'Port Townsend Public Library, 1220 Lawrence Street, run by the City of Port Townsend. Hours not checked.',
  [NR('82004908'),L('Peninsula Daily News: library reopens in its Carnegie building (2014)','https://www.peninsuladailynews.com/news/port-townsend-library-turns-a-chapter-with-reopening-of-its-carnegie-location/'),L('Port Townsend Leader: Carnegie library at 110','https://ptleader.com/stories/pt-carnegie-library-celebrates-110th-anniversary,144196')],
  brick='?'),
N('st-pauls-episcopal-port-townsend','St. Paul\'s Episcopal Church','Port Townsend','wa','worship','standing',48.115556,-122.757778,'exact','view','walk','','1865',
  'Built in 1865, when of the three Episcopal parishes in Washington Territory only Port Townsend had a church; the bills include stained glass bought in Victoria that year. In 1883 it was put on rollers and moved to its present site. Its bell was given by a ship\'s captain who said the rector\'s hand-bell, rung in fog, had kept him off the rocks; it was given on condition it be rung in fog to guide ships in.',
  'A white clapboard church with a shingled bell tower and the bell in its open belfry, and stained-glass windows either side of the red door (Wikimedia Commons photograph opened in this session). Inside, the memorial window over the altar added in the late 1890s, when the chancel was rebuilt to fit it (form, 1970). Whether the 1865 glass survives and when the church is open were not checked.',
  'The church bell that was rung to bring ships in through the fog.',
  'An Episcopal parish church; seen from the street. Service times and access to the interior not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('St. Paul\'s Episcopal Church (Port Townsend, Washington)'),NR('70000640')],
  brick='no'),
N('starrett-house-port-townsend','Starrett House (House of the Four Seasons)','Port Townsend','wa','landmark','standing',48.11790,-122.75797,'exact','view','walk','','1889',
  'Built in 1889 by George E. Starrett, a contractor from Maine who came to Port Townsend in 1885. He hired the artist George Chapman to paint the four seasons on the panels of the dome inside the tower, which gave the house its other name.',
  'Carpenter Gothic outside, with ornament on every gable. Inside (form, 1970): the painted dome seen from the entrance hall, a free-hung spiral staircase of two full turns with banisters of five woods, carved corner medallions, and fruit and flower frescoes uncovered when false ceilings were removed. It is private; whether it now takes guests or opens for tours was not checked.',
  'A spiral staircase climbing to a dome painted with the four seasons.',
  'Private, at 744 Clay Street; seen from the street. Street parking.',
  [NR('70000641')],
  brick='?'),
N('manresa-castle','Manresa Castle (Eisenbeis Castle)','Port Townsend','wa','hotel','standing',48.10448,-122.78988,'site','view','drive','hotels','1892',
  'Charles Eisenbeis, a Prussian baker who ran a brewery, a brickyard and a lumber mill and put up much of the downtown, built this 30-room house in 1892 at the peak of the boom, of 12-inch walls of brick from his own works (PCAD). It stood empty for almost twenty years after his death in 1902. The Jesuits made it a house for priests in their last year of training and in 1928 added a wing with a chapel; about then the brick was stuccoed over. Sources disagree on the Jesuits\' purchase: 1925 on the Register form, 1927 in PCAD and the Port Townsend Leader, which say a Seattle lawyer bought it in 1925 as a retreat for teaching nuns. The Jesuits sold it in 1968 and it became an inn.',
  'A stuccoed "Rhine castle" front with turrets on the hill above Sims Way. The form (1970) describes redwood and oak panelling, tile fireplaces, and a chapel in the 1928 wing with leaded stained glass and 18-foot ceilings; what is still in place inside was not checked.',
  'A brick-maker\'s castle that became a Jesuit house and then a hotel.',
  'A private hotel off Sheridan Street. In 2016 it was sold to H&R Hospitality, which said it would go on running it as a hotel (Port Townsend Leader); current opening not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [NR('70000638'),L('PCAD: Manresa Castle','https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/building/8108'),L('Port Townsend Leader: Manresa Castle sold (2016)','https://www.ptleader.com/stories/manresa-castle-sells-for-19m,17325')],
  brick='no'),
N('irondale-iron-works','Irondale iron and steel works','Port Hadlock','wa','industry','ruins',48.04452,-122.76851,'site','public','walk','','1881–1919',
  'The Puget Sound Iron Company blew in a charcoal blast furnace here on January 27, 1881, smelting bog ore dug along Chimacum Creek; it was the second iron furnace on the Pacific coast. Twenty charcoal kilns 30 feet across were built along the bay. The works closed after 1889, came back in 1902, and in 1910 added steel, the first plant west of Denver to make both, before the Western Steel Corporation went bankrupt at the end of 1911. The furnace ran again from 1917 to 1919 and was then taken down; that ended pig iron making in Washington.',
  'Foundations of the plant and of the charcoal kilns, left in place on purpose when the state cleaned up the site; the 1983 Register form names the foundation work of three open-hearth furnaces and their smokestack holes. Four company houses of the district are private. The small 1911 Irondale jail stood at the foot of Moore Street in 1983, then decaying; whether it is there now was not checked.',
  'The furnace that made the last pig iron in Washington.',
  'Irondale Beach County Park, Jefferson County, at the foot of Moore Street (526 Moore Street on the Department of Ecology page). Reopened after the cleanup with a ribbon-cutting on January 24 (year on the Ecology page not read). Hours not checked. The pin is the park\'s lot at the end of East Moore Street, by the beach.',
  [NR('83003326'),L('Department of Ecology: Irondale Iron & Steel Plant cleanup','https://apps.ecology.wa.gov/gsp/Sitepage.aspx?csid=4484'),L('Jefferson County: Irondale Beach Park master plan (2004)','https://jeffersoncountywa.myrec.com/forms/3152_IrondaleBeachPark.pdf')],
  brick='?'),
N('enchanted-valley-chalet','Enchanted Valley Chalet','Quinault','wa','hotel','standing',47.67135,-123.389,'exact','public','hike','hotels','1931',
  'A backcountry lodge of hewn silver-fir logs built in 1931 by Tom Criswell, his son Glen and the Olsons of Quinault, whose company ran it for hikers and horse parties 13 miles up the Quinault valley. It closed as a lodge in 1943, was an aircraft-warning post in the Second World War, and passed to the Park Service, which bought it in 1939. It was restored in 1983-84 and again in 2010. The East Fork of the Quinault River cut toward it, and in September 2014 it was dragged 50 to 100 feet back from the bank; it is still at risk.',
  'The two-and-a-half-storey log chalet in a meadow under the valley walls, with a shake roof. It is no longer a lodge; the Park Service trail page (updated May 2026) says it comes into view just after the bridge before the Enchanted Valley camp. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1930s log hotel 13 miles from any road that the river is trying to take.',
  'Olympic National Park. Thirteen miles one way on the East Fork Quinault River Trail from the Graves Creek trailhead, usually two or three days out and back. A wilderness permit is needed to camp and bear canisters are required; Road to the trailhead not checked.',
  [W('Enchanted Valley Chalet'),L('NPS: East Fork Quinault River Trail','https://www.nps.gov/olym/planyourvisit/east-fork-quinault-river-trail.htm')],
  th=[47.57267,-123.57077],thn='Graves Creek trailhead',brick='no'),
]
